95
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:40,000
unsuccessful hunters. Those people in turn preserved knowledge and labor that made the next hunt more likely to

96
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:51,000
succeed. The true northern adaptation was therefore not thick skin. It was other humans. Modern humans are unusually capable of cumulative culture.

97
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:58,000
A useful technique can be copied, improved, taught, and passed beyond the life of its inventor. No child needs to

98
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:06,000
rediscover sewing by freezing in several experimental outfits. Language would have amplified this system. Detailed

99
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:14,000
communication could describe distant locations, animal movements, dangers, and procedures. Stories could preserve

100
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:22,000
seasonal knowledge. Social rules could organize sharing, marriage, visiting, and conflict.

101
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:34,000
Archaeological sites hint at connections extending far beyond one camp. At Jana in northeastern Siberia, people used stone, ivory, bone, and other materials.

102
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:40,000
While ornaments and some non-local resources suggest mobility or wider networks, such networks were not

103
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:48,000
decorative extras. They were survival infrastructure. Small isolated populations face serious problems. A run

104
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:57,000
of deaths can erase specialized knowledge. Inbreeding can increase. A local herd can change its route. Tools

105
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:06,000
break. A group may need partners, information, materials, or somewhere else to go. Maintaining ties across

106
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:14,000
distance gave people options. The North did not reward complete independence. It rewarded organized dependence stretched

107
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:21,000
across the landscape. The Jana site makes this visible. Located around 71°

108
