north in northeastern Siberia, it dates to roughly 32,000 years ago. Excavations

109
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:37,000
have produced stone tools, bone and ivory objects, weapons, animal remains, and ornaments. Human teeth recovered

110
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:44,000
there later yielded DNA from a population researchers call ancient North Siberians.

111
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:51,000
These were not desperate visitors, leaving one confused footprint. The density and variety of material indicate

112
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:58,000
repeated or sustained activity by people who understood the environment. They hunted and processed animals,

113
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:05,000
manufactured complex equipment, and participated in a cultural world rich enough to include beads and decoration.

114
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:13,000
Apparently, surviving Arctic winter was not considered a sufficient hobby. They also made art. An even older and more

115
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:20,000
dramatic clue comes from a mammoth carcass found near the Siberian Arctic coast. Cut marks and injuries

116
