159
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:08,000
environments while remaining one species. Humans did not evolve fur every time they entered a cold place or gills whenever they approached a coast. They

160
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:17,000
carried flexible technology, cooperation, and information. Our bodies remained broadly tropical. Our behavior

161
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:24,000
became planetary. The northern expansion also changed world history in a way none of its participants could have

162
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:32,000
predicted. During colder periods, so much water was locked in ice sheets that global sea levels fell. The shallow

163
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:41,000
seabed between Siberia and Alaska emerged as Bingia. It is often imagined as a narrow icy bridge. In reality,

164
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:50,000
Bingia was at times a vast region containing step tundra environments, animals, rivers, and human populations.

165
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:58,000
People moving through it were not necessarily attempting to discover America. They were living within a landscape that continued eastward.

166
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:02,000
Eventually, descendants of Northeast Asian populations entered the Americas.

167
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:09,000
Although the timing, routes, and population history remain active areas of research.

168
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:17,000
Coastal and interior movements may both have mattered at different stages. The journey into the frozen north therefore did more than expand the edge of

169
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:26,000
habitation. It helped place humans beside another continent. Still, it would be wrong to treat the Arctic as merely a hallway to somewhere important.

170
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:34,000
For the people living there, it was home. The ornaments at Yana, the careful tools, and the repeated processing of

171
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:40,000
animals show lives richer than a survival montage. People formed relationships, raised children, learned

172
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:49,000
places, decorated bodies and objects, probably argued about decisions that now survive only as scattered bone. The

173
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:57,000
North became human when it acquired memory. A riverbend was no longer just geography. It was where reindeer crossed

174
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:05,000
last autumn. A ridge was where someone found stone. A sheltered hollow belonged to a story. Once knowledge accumulated,

175
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:13,000
newcomers were not facing untouched wilderness. They were inheriting a cultural landscape. That inheritance

176
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:20,000
explains the deepest answer to the question. Ancient humans entered the frozen north because previous humans had

177
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:27,000
made the next step possible. One generation learned how to scrape hides more efficiently. Another improved a

178
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:34,000
point. Someone discovered a dependable fuel source. Someone survived a storm because a companion shared food. Someone

179
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:45,000
remembered the herd had crossed farther east during a dry year. No single breakthrough conquered cold. Thousands of small improvements formed a system.

180
